course: dictionary
lesson: 6007
title: Meconium, a newborn's first poo
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Meconium is a special word for the very first poo of a newborn baby. It is dark green, thick and sticky, and it usually comes out in the first day or two after birth.
explain: Where does it come from? Before it is born, a baby swallows a little of the fluid around it in the womb. What the baby swallows stays in its gut, and it leaves the body as meconium.
explain: Doctors and midwives check for meconium because it shows that the baby's gut is working. The phrase to pass meconium means a baby has done this first poo.
explain: Context helps here too. «A new butterfly pumps fluid into its crumpled wings until they open and dry; the red drop below it is meconium, leftover waste, not blood.» The clues leftover waste and not blood tell you that meconium is waste, not blood.
fact: Meconium means the first poo of a newborn baby.
fact: Meconium is dark green, thick and sticky.
fact: Meconium is made from what the baby swallowed in the womb.
fact: Midwives and doctors check for meconium to make sure the baby's gut is working.
fact: The phrase «pass meconium» means a baby has done its first poo.
exercise: What is meconium? | *The first poo of a newborn baby | A toy that babies chew | A sleepy noise a baby makes | A kind of baby food
exercise: In the butterfly sentence, the clues «leftover waste» and «not blood» show that meconium is what? | *Waste | A new wing | A kind of blood | A type of food
exercise: Why do doctors and midwives check for meconium? | To see if the baby can sing | To count the baby's teeth | *To make sure the baby's gut is working | To give the baby a name
test: What colour is meconium? | Bright pink | Sky blue | *Dark green | Pure white
test: Which phrase means a baby has done its first poo? | Blow out the candles | *Pass meconium | Make a wish | Pass the salt
exam: Where does meconium come from? | The sun | A bee's sting | The wind | *What the baby swallowed in the womb
